ur new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nEditing before revising can polish a broken ending. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Edit surface features second"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying ending independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail."],"question":"Which is the strongest next step when learning to name one revision and one edit?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\nRevision: I linked the latch repair to the escape. Edit: I capitalised Dad.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nIdentify one meaning change and one punctuation fix in your lost-map ending.\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\nThe meaning change should affect the solution; the edit should affect spelling or punctuation.","explain":"Show that you understand the difference between changing ideas and fixing conventions.\n\nShow that you understand the difference between changing ideas and fixing conventions. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT